WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:20.120
Graduates of the Class of 2021,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.622
family members, and friends,

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.691
it is a privilege to
speak with you as

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.260
part of this
remarkable moment.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.929
You are probably feeling many
emotions right now.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.899
I often tell graduates that
commencement is a field day for

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:37.203
me as a psychologist, and that
has never been more true.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.640
For over a year, we
have walked a strange and

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.343
historic path together.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:47.781
We are now at the end of an
academic year like no other.

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:50.750
Although our traditions may
look different this year,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.220
they are just as
meaningful as ever.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:57.490
Commencement is a
time of celebration.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:01.828
It is a chance for you,
our graduates, to revel

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:04.030
in your accomplishments.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.302
It is also an opportunity to
reflect and say thank you.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:13.173
So many people have been part
of your journey to this moment.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:16.376
I hope you will find time to
tell them how much they have

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.511
meant to you.

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:23.249
I also would like to
say thank you, to you.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.687
Graduates of Yale College,
you have made history at Yale.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.124
Many of you have studied, and
learned, and made a community

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.160
here under
extraordinary conditions.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.697
Others have studied remotely
from around the world.

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:42.368
Day after day, for more
than a year.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:46.406
You have made sacrifices for
the greater good, helping keep

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:49.676
our neighbors on campus
and in New Haven safe.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:52.112
It has not been easy.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.881
Your "bright college years"
have not always been

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.950
what you expected.

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:01.788
Like people around the world,
you have experienced loss,

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.591
grief, and uncertainty.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.060
And yet,

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.297
you have repaid our communities'
trust and confidence in you

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.967
many times over.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:18.605
Today, as we look to
the future, I would like to

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:23.676
share a personal story about my
past and my present.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:27.247
I share it because I
hope it will shed some light

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.649
on your future.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:34.787
It is a story about teachers
and the gifts they give us.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.791
In the early 1970s,
I was a student at

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:41.394
Williamsville
High School North

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:43.830
outside of Buffalo, New York.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.500
It was a pretty typical large
public high school.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.369
We had a popular football team

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:52.739
and a marching band that 
played at the games.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:56.142
Perhaps it will surprise you to
learn that I did not play on

00:02:56.142 --> 00:03:00.647
the football team, but I did
march with the band.

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:04.350
Now, you might wonder how I fit
into a school like this.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.653
I actually earned
a varsity letter

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:08.988
for the drama club.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:12.792
I was part of another student
organization as well,

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:16.529
a poetry club hosted by
two beloved English teachers,

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.265
Mr. and Mrs. Blaisdell.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.802
A group of us would gather in
their home and share poems we

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:25.438
had written or come across.

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:30.210
We drank coffee, we talked
about poetry, it was all very

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.214
grown up, especially for kids
like us from Buffalo.

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:38.451
Our teachers were only in
their 20s, perhaps early 30s,

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.955
but they were sophisticated
adults and therefore seemed

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:46.559
much older to us. They cared
deeply about literature,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.962
writing, and poetry.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:53.800
And most of all, they cared
about us, their students.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.636
In fact, I'm not the only
student of the Blaisdells

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.906
on the faculty here at Yale:
they taught English to

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.774
Chemistry Professor
Scott Miller

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:04.110
about a decade after me.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.480
Fast forward forty-six years.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:12.151
The kids from that poetry club
now have families and careers

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:13.753
of their own.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.955
We have stayed in touch,

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:21.060
but usually we only see each
other every few years at best.

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.130
As a way of coping
with the social isolation of

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.433
the pandemic, we started
meeting over Zoom.

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:32.005
We shared poems, we drank
coffee together again.

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:35.241
Our former English teachers
from Williamsville North,

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:38.244
now retired, joined us.

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:42.548
And we found in the midst of so
much grief and loneliness,

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:45.018
a way to connect.

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:50.857
What does this mean for you,
the graduates of 2021?

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:54.827
First, my hope for each of you
is that you have made a friend

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.563
here at Yale, perhaps
a few friends

00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.566
who will be there for you
in fourty-six years.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.337
That is no small thing.

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:08.975
Second, I hope you have found
great teachers and developed

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:12.912
interests that you will take
with you for the rest

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.580
of your lives.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.383
I don't mean only
your professional interests,

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:20.186
many of you, without question,
have found important mentors

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:24.724
who will continue to advise and
guide you, but you have also

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:27.360
found interests at Yale that
have nothing to do

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.995
with your careers.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:33.099
For me, it was poetry,
theater, and music.

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:36.469
But for you, it may have been
an economics class or a

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:40.073
history seminar that made you
re-examine the world.

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:43.543
It might have been a certain
psychology class that helped

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:46.312
you think about happiness
in new ways.

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:49.515
Or maybe you heard a lecture or
attended an event that sparked

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:53.920
an interest in the
outdoors, or food, or dance.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.955
These moments of inspiration
and learning

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:57.824
can happen anywhere.

00:05:57.824 --> 00:06:01.728
We now know that they
can even happen on Zoom.

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:04.497
My friends from
Williamsville North

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.933
have gone into music, science,

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.802
medicine, law, and theater.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:13.606
I'm a psychology professor
and university leader.

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:18.811
My point is, none of us are
professional poets,

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:23.583
and yet our experiences in the
poetry club were transformative.

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:27.053
From wonderful teachers,
I learned how to ask

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:30.156
different questions,
bigger questions.

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:32.358
In high school, I was always
trying to figure out what a

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:34.093
poem was "about".

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:37.730
I wanted to unlock
the secret meaning.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.565
But Mr. Blaisdell would tell me,

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:42.068
"Peter, what the poem is about
is not always

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.537
the most interesting question."

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:48.441
He encouraged me to appreciate
beauty and occasionally

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:50.777
put aside analysis.

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:53.346
He pushed me to use different
parts of my mind

00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:55.548
and my emotions.

00:06:56.182 --> 00:07:00.086
Beyond poetry, my teachers
inspired in me and my friends,

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:03.689
a deep and joyful
love of learning.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:07.527
They introduced us to interests
that made our lives richer

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:09.896
and more rewarding.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:13.366
I recently had the privilege of
interviewing the poet

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:18.337
Louise Glück, who won the
2020 Nobel Prize for Literature.

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:21.841
Professor Glück has taught at
Yale since 2004.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:24.343
She told me she thinks of
teaching as a way of

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.379
repaying her debts.

00:07:26.379 --> 00:07:30.716
"You can't thank your teachers,"
she said, "except by performing

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:34.220
as they did for
another generation."

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:40.193
Soon you will leave Yale and
contribute to every field

00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:44.163
and profession, as generations
of alumni have before you.

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:49.202
You will leave here
with gifts from your teachers,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:50.870
from Yale professors,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.639
and from others
throughout your lives.

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:58.377
And as Professor Glück says,
you can repay your teachers

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:01.781
by helping others
discover the world.

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:05.318
Over the past year, we have
learned so much about

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:09.422
ourselves and about what we
are capable of.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:13.292
And we have been reminded again
and again that we

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.228
must keep learning.

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:19.465
There will always be new
challenges to tackle,

00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:22.335
unexpected problems to solve.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.937
We will have
to pivot and adapt.

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:29.108
And if we love learning, if we
are curious about the world and

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:33.279
the people in it, then that
task will be so much easier

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:35.882
and more fulfilling.

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:41.654
What will you do with
the gifts you have been given?

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:46.192
What gifts will you
give to the world?

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:51.931
I think of Emi Mahmoud,
a 2016 graduate of Yale College,

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:54.734
born in Darfur, Sudan, Emi
won the

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:58.437
International World 
Poetry Slam Championship,

00:08:58.437 --> 00:09:00.506
was honored by the BBC,

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:02.708
and met with President Obama,

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:05.711
all while still
a student at Yale.

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:08.548
Emi's poems often
deal with her experiences of

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:11.350
war and displacement.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.954
Today, as the United Nations
goodwill ambassador,

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:18.057
she advocates for peace and
human rights,

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:20.660
particularly the
rights of children.

00:09:21.127 --> 00:09:23.362
She is sharing her gifts
with the world

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.498
in a very powerful way.

00:09:26.032 --> 00:09:28.034
In the book
Tuesdays with Morrie,

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:31.337
The sportswriter Mitch Albom
describes reconnecting with his

00:09:31.337 --> 00:09:34.340
former professor,
Morrie Schwartz.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.044
The elderly Morrie
is suffering with ALS,

00:09:38.044 --> 00:09:40.479
Lou Gehrig's disease.

00:09:40.479 --> 00:09:44.917
The book is an extended
reflection on life and death,

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:48.554
but it is also about the
relationship between a student

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:50.456
and his teacher.

00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:53.292
At the end, Albom says,

00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:56.362
"Have you ever really
had a teacher?

00:09:56.362 --> 00:09:59.165
One who saw you as a
raw but precious thing, 

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:01.601
a jewel that, with wisdom,

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.837
could be polished
to a proud shine?

00:10:04.837 --> 00:10:08.374
If you are lucky enough to find
your way to such teachers,

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:11.310
you will always find
your way back."

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.080
I hope you find your way back.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.483
Back to your teachers and
back to Yale.

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:20.920
I hope you will return to campus
and walk down Chapel Street

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.255
and enjoy the sunshine,
or the snow,

00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:26.559
on Old Campus or Science Hill.

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:30.196
And I hope you will find ways
of sharing the joy of

00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:32.365
discovery with others.

00:10:32.365 --> 00:10:35.701
I hope you will share your
curiosity with a world that

00:10:35.701 --> 00:10:41.207
needs questions as much as
answers,

00:10:41.207 --> 00:10:44.710
that needs "light and
truth" more than ever.

00:10:45.778 --> 00:10:49.849
I usually conclude my addresses
on commencement weekend with a

00:10:49.849 --> 00:10:53.519
few words of verse. Over
the years, I have read from

00:10:53.519 --> 00:10:56.956
Rabindranath Tagore,
Elizabeth Alexander,

00:10:56.956 --> 00:11:01.727
Billy Collins, Marie Borroff,
and Claudia Rankine.

00:11:01.727 --> 00:11:05.498
But most often I have turned
to the words of John Milton

00:11:05.498 --> 00:11:08.100
from Paradise Lost.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.804
You know those verses about
leaving the Garden of Eden

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:15.741
"with wandering steps and slow" 
making one's way into a world

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:18.344
that is "all before" you?

00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:21.947
There are moments when those
lines move me to tears.

00:11:22.648 --> 00:11:26.052
In this challenging year,
though, paradise does not seem

00:11:26.052 --> 00:11:29.221
the right metaphor for
our beloved campus.

00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:32.692
But I look ahead with
great optimism,

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:35.428
and I trust you do too.

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:38.564
So perhaps these words of
Emily Dickinson

00:11:38.564 --> 00:11:42.134
seem more appropriate at
this moment.

00:11:42.134 --> 00:11:44.336
"Hope is the
thing with feathers

00:11:44.336 --> 00:11:46.806
That perches in the soul

00:11:46.806 --> 00:11:49.942
And sings the tune
without the words

00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:53.112
And never stops, 
at all"

00:11:54.313 --> 00:11:57.650
Graduates of the
Class of 2021,

00:11:58.584 --> 00:12:00.586
You are ready to
face the future with

00:12:00.586 --> 00:12:02.855
optimism and determination.

00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:06.659
You are ready to repay the
gifts your teachers have given

00:12:06.659 --> 00:12:10.596
you and share the joy of
discovery with others.

00:12:10.596 --> 00:12:15.401
You go with our best wishes
and our admiration.

00:12:15.735 --> 00:12:19.972
Congratulations, and good luck!

